<h2 id="accum-heading">Accumulation &amp; Merge Conveyors</h2>
<h3>Zero-Pressure Accumulation</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Zero-pressure accumulation conveyors</a> stage products without contact between items. Each zone holds one product and stops independently. When a downstream zone clears, the upstream zone releases its product. This prevents product damage, label scuffing, and case crushing that occurs when products press against each other under back-pressure.</p>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR (motor-driven roller) conveyors</a> are the standard zero-pressure accumulation technology in modern distribution centers. Each zone contains a motorized roller that drives 3-7 slave rollers through O-ring belts. Photoeye sensors detect product presence and control zone release. MDR zones consume power only when product is present, reducing energy costs by 50-70% compared to continuously running belt conveyors.</p>
<h3>Merge Conveyors</h3>
<p>Merge conveyors combine flows from multiple pick zones, pack stations, or parallel conveyors onto a single outbound line. The merge must insert items into gaps on the main line without collision or stoppage. Merge technologies:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Speed-up merge:</strong> The merging conveyor accelerates to match the main line speed and inserts items into natural gaps. Simplest and lowest cost. Works when main line has sufficient gap density.</li>
<li><strong>Slug merge:</strong> Holds multiple items on the merge conveyor, then releases them as a group when a gap opens on the main line. Higher throughput than one-at-a-time merging.</li>
<li><strong>Servo-controlled merge:</strong> Servo drives precisely time individual item release to insert into specific gaps on the main line. Highest throughput and most reliable but most expensive.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="asrs-heading">AS/RS &amp; AMR Integration</h2>
<p>Automated Storage and Retrieval Systems (AS/RS) and Autonomous Mobile Robots (AMRs) connect to the conveyor network through transfer points, staging conveyors, and accumulation zones. The conveyor system is the fixed backbone, AS/RS and AMR systems are the flexible storage and transport layer.</p>
<h3>AS/RS Conveyor Interface</h3>
<p>Conveyors connect to AS/RS cranes and shuttles at the end-of-aisle transfer point. Infeed conveyors deliver loads (cases, totes, pallets) to the AS/RS for storage. Outfeed conveyors receive retrieved loads for picking or shipping. Accumulation zones buffer loads between the conveyor's continuous flow and the AS/RS crane's cycle-based operation. Sizing the <a href="/conveyor-types/buffer-conveyors/">accumulation buffer</a> correctly prevents bottlenecks, the buffer must absorb the AS/RS cycle time variation without starving downstream operations.</p>
<p>Dematic, TGW, Swisslog, and Honeywell Intelligrated supply integrated AS/RS and conveyor systems. For standalone conveyor systems connecting to third-party AS/RS, the interface specification must define load dimensions, transfer height, conveyor speed, handshake signals, and communication protocol (typically Ethernet/IP or PROFINET).</p>
<h3>AMR Conveyor Interface</h3>
<p>AMR integration uses conveyor-mounted load/unload stations where AMRs pick up or deliver totes and cases. The station includes a short conveyor section, presence sensors, and a communication interface (Wi-Fi, 5G, or infrared) that coordinates the AMR docking cycle with conveyor motion. AMR providers like 6 River Systems (Shopify), Locus Robotics, and Geek+ integrate with standard conveyor systems through WCS (Warehouse Control System) software.</p>
<h2 id="elevation-heading">Vertical Transport in Distribution</h2>
<p>Multi-level distribution centers and mezzanine operations need vertical conveyors to move cases, totes, and pallets between levels. Three technologies serve this need:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Spiral conveyors ($$$):</strong> Continuous-flow spirals move cases and totes through elevation changes without stopping. Compact footprint. Speeds up to 200 FPM. See also: <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/cooling/">spiral cooling conveyors</a> for food applications.</li>
<li><strong><a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/">Vertical reciprocating conveyors (VRCs)</a> ($$-$$$):</strong> Platform-based lifts that carry cases or pallets between levels. Cycle-based (not continuous). Suited for lower-throughput, heavy-load applications.</li>
<li><strong>Incline/decline belt conveyors ($$):</strong> Belt conveyors at 15-30 degree incline. Simple and low cost for moderate elevation changes. Limited to products that do not slide on the inclined belt surface.</li>
</ul>
<p>For layout and cost optimization, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> and <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated systems guide</a>.</p>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What conveyors are used in warehouses?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Belt conveyors, <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyors</a>, sortation (shoe, crossbelt, tilt-tray), <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation</a>, merge, and vertical conveyors. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a> are standard for modern zero-pressure accumulation.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is a sortation conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A sortation conveyor automatically diverts products to designated lanes based on barcode or RFID data. Types include sliding shoe (gentle, high-speed), crossbelt (widest product range), pop-up wheel (lower-speed), and tilt-tray (small items). Speeds: 100-600+ FPM.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What is AS/RS conveyor integration?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Conveyors connect AS/RS (Automated Storage/Retrieval Systems) to picking and shipping. Infeed conveyors deliver loads for storage; outfeed conveyors receive retrieved loads. Accumulation zones buffer between continuous conveyor flow and AS/RS cycle timing.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How do merge conveyors work?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Merge conveyors combine multiple streams into one line using speed-up merges (accelerate into gaps), slug merges (release groups), or servo-controlled merges (precise individual timing). Servo merges provide highest throughput and reliability.</p>

</div>
